course: dictionary
lesson: 6408
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. Mushroom can be a noun, a fungus with a stalk and a round cap, or a verb, meaning to grow or spread very fast. The sentence around the word shows you which meaning is used.
explain: Read the clues. «New houses mushroomed in the town.» Houses are not fungi, and they cannot grow like a mushroom does, so mushroomed must mean spread or grew very fast. Try your meaning in the sentence: New houses grew very fast in the town. It still makes sense.
explain: Now read «Some wild mushrooms are deadly poisonous, so we must never eat ones we pick ourselves.» The clues are wild, poisonous and eat, so mushrooms here means the fungus. Always ask which meaning fits the words around it.
fact: A mushroom is a fungus with a stalk and a round cap; some kinds are eaten as food.
fact: Mushrooms are not plants. They are fungi that feed on rotting things.
fact: As a verb, «mushroomed» means grew or spread very fast.
fact: Some wild mushrooms are deadly poisonous and look like safe ones, so never eat ones you pick yourself. Only eat mushrooms from a shop.
fact: The mushroom we see is only the fruit of a fungus. Most of the fungus is a web of fine threads hidden in soil or wood, and it spreads tiny spores instead of seeds.
exercise: «Some wild mushrooms are deadly poisonous.» What is a mushroom? | A small green plant that makes its own food from sunlight | A bowl used for making soup | *A fungus with a stalk and a round cap | A tall smoking pipe with water in it
exercise: «New houses mushroomed in the town.» What does mushroomed mean here? | *Grew or spread very fast | Fell down slowly | Were painted bright green | Turned into a fungus
exercise: Why must we never eat wild mushrooms that we pick ourselves? | Because they only grow in shops | *Because some are very poisonous and look like safe ones | Because they taste too sweet to finish | Because they are a kind of cake
test: Mushrooms spread tiny spores instead of what? | Leaves | *Seeds | Pollen | Roots
test: Mushrooms are not plants. What do they feed on? | *Rotting things | Sunlight | Water from the rain | Sugar from the shop
exam: Maya says, «Mushrooms are plants, so they make their own food from sunlight.» What is wrong with her idea? | Mushrooms make their food in the dark, so they are plants | Mushrooms are plants that grow in the sun | *Mushrooms are fungi, not plants, and they feed on rotting things | Mushrooms are a kind of tree
